Let me think carefully about test cases for the requirement.

Requirement: `id` is `DD-MM-xxxxxx-YYYY`: day, month, year entry written, six random lowercase letters and digits. Example `13-10-k3f7a2-2026`. Writers on different branches pick ids without coordinating, so id is never "next free number". No two entries share an id. Id never changes, so file keeps its name.

Fails if: id not in W1.1 form, two entries share an id, or git shows an entry file renamed to a different id.

Context: each entry is one Markdown file in a project memory folder, named `<prefix>-<id>-<slug>.md`, with id also in file header. A checker tool reads the folder and reports violations.

So test cases: inputs/situations and expected results (violation or no violation). Cover:
- valid id form: `13-10-k3f7a2-2026`
- valid with digits in random part, lowercase letters
- date components: day DD (01-31), month MM (01-12), year YYYY (4 digits)
- random part: exactly 6 chars, lowercase a-z and 0-9, e.g. all digits, all letters, mixed
- invalid: uppercase letters in random part
- invalid: fewer than 6 chars
- invalid: more than 6
- invalid: hyphen inside random part
- invalid: special chars
- invalid: uppercase
- invalid: day single digit / month single digit
- invalid: year two digits
- invalid: separators wrong (slashes, dots)
- invalid: empty
- duplicate ids across two files
- duplicate id within header vs filename mismatch
- filename must contain id
- header id must match filename id
- git rename detection: file renamed to different id → violation
- git shows rename but same id (slug change) → maybe no violation? The requirement says "an id never changes, so the file keeps its name" — but the failure condition is "git shows an entry file renamed to a different id." A slug rename within the same id would not be a different id. Hmm, "the file keeps its name" implies renaming to a different slug would also be a change but failure condition specifically says renamed to a different id. Test: rename slug only, same id → no violation per stated failure condition? That's ambiguous; maybe include as a case with expected result based on the stated failure condition.

Also: two entries share id but in different branches/not merged? Checker reads folder, only sees one. Requirement: no two entries share an id — checker reads the folder. Test: two files in the folder with same id → violation. Cross-branch duplicates aren't visible to checker.

Also: entry written on a different date than id date? The id encodes the day/month/year the entry is written. Is a mismatch a failure? Not listed in failure conditions. Could include a case: id date differs from file's git creation date → not a violation under the stated checks? Hmm, the failure list is exhaustive-ish ("It fails if: ..."). We should base expected results on those three. But "in the W1.1 form" might include semantic date validity (e.g., day 32, month 13, Feb 30). That's part of the form arguably. Test: `32-13-abcdef-2026` → violation (not a valid DD-MM).

Also "six random lowercase letters and digits" — need at least checking character set. Could test random part containing `_` or `-`.

Also file naming: `<prefix>-<id>-<slug>.md`. Prefix unknown. Test: file whose name lacks the id → violation? The failure conditions only mention id form, duplicate, rename. Hmm. A file name not containing a well-formed id means the entry's id isn't in W1.1 form? Or missing. I'd include: file with no id in filename / header missing id → violation.

Header: id also in file header. Test: id in filename valid but header has different id → violation (id not consistent / not in form). Test: header id valid matches filename → pass.

